# WordJS code owners.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR. One thing, and it is the review ledger. `marketplace/reviews.json` is the only
# tracked file in this repository whose contents are a CLAIM ABOUT A HUMAN DECISION: a record in it is
# what puts the "Reviewed" badge on a catalog entry, on the highest-privilege screen in the product,
# immediately above an Install button. Every other check in the marketplace pipeline verifies that the
# artefacts agree with each other; nothing in a diff can verify WHO wrote a line of the ledger.
#
# The ledger is therefore owned, and a change to it requires the owner's review. The mechanical half of
# the same rule lives in .github/workflows/plugin-review.yml, which refuses a pull request that edits a
# plugin package AND the ledger together — a submitter cannot record their own review even in a diff
# nobody reads closely. Ownership is the other half: it decides who may record one at all.
#
# HOW THIS BECOMES ENFORCEMENT. CODEOWNERS by itself only REQUESTS a review. It becomes a gate when
# "Require review from Code Owners" is enabled on the protected branch — and that setting is meaningful
# only once a second maintainer exists, because GitHub does not let an author approve their own pull
# request: with a single maintainer, requiring code-owner review on a file that maintainer edits would
# block every change to it, including a legitimate review record. So the setting is turned on with the
# second maintainer account, and until then this file's job is to make the ownership explicit, put the
# ledger on the owner's review queue automatically, and record what the branch protection must become.
# marketplace/REVIEW.md §8 says the same thing in the policy, where an outside author will read it.
#
# Syntax: last matching pattern wins. Paths are anchored with a leading slash where a repository-root
# match is meant, so `/marketplace/reviews.json` is that one file and nothing else named like it.

# The review ledger — the badge itself.
/marketplace/reviews.json           @jaimemartinez

